\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A coupled interacting coordinate retains memory}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Take the two-site specialization of the coupled chain in \ref{fdip-00GV} and
\ref{fdip-00HD}. Retain #{x} from #{\mathsf Q=\mathbb R^2}, with}

##{\begin{aligned}
U(x,y)&=\frac{m^2}{2}(x^2+y^2)+\frac c2(x-y)^2\\
 &\quad+\frac{\lambda_x}{24}x^4+\frac{\lambda_y}{24}y^4,
\end{aligned}}

\p{where #{m,c>0}, #{\lambda_x,\lambda_y\geq0} and at least one quartic coupling is
positive. Then #{U\geq(m^2/2)(x^2+y^2)}, but the compressed family #{A_t} is not a
semigroup.}

\p{Write #{\psi=\psi_0}. The form domain in \ref{fdip-00H2} gives #{\psi\in
H^1_{\mathrm{loc}}}. Suppose #{\psi\in H^k_{\mathrm{loc}}}, #{k\ge1}. Its weak
equation #{\covsq\psi=2(U-E_0)\psi} has right side in #{H^k_{\mathrm{loc}}} because
#{U} is smooth. For a smooth compactly supported cutoff #{\chi},}

##{\covsq(\chi\psi)=\chi\covsq\psi+2\cov\chi\cdot\cov\psi+(\covsq\chi)\psi\in H^{k-1}.}

\p{Fourier transformation gives #{\chi\psi\in H^{k+1}}: the weight
#{(1+|\xi|^2)^{k+1}} is bounded by a constant times #{1+|\xi|^4(1+|\xi|^2)^{k-1}}.
Nested cutoffs and induction yield every local Sobolev order. For each derivative
order #{j}, choose #{k>j+1}; Cauchy–Schwarz makes #{|\xi|^j\widehat{\chi\psi}}
integrable. Fourier inversion gives continuous derivatives through order #{j}. Thus
#{\psi} is smooth. Its representative is strictly positive by \ref{fdip-00H6}.}

\p{For #{f\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R)}, the function
#{w(x,y)=\psi(x,y)f(x)} lies in the form domain: #{f,f'} are bounded,
#{\psi,\cov\psi\in L^2}, and the potential-weighted integral is bounded by
#{\|f\|_\infty^2} times the corresponding integral for #{\psi}. In distributions,}

##{ (H-E_0)w=-\tfrac12\psi f''-(\partial_x\psi)f'.}

\p{The right side is in #{L^2}. The identity initially paired with compactly supported
smooth test functions extends to the whole form domain by the form-core density in
\ref{fdip-00H2}. The form characterization of #{D(H)} therefore gives #{w\in D(H)}
with this action. Compact support in the eliminated coordinate is unnecessary.}

\p{Suppose that #{A_t} were a semigroup. \ref{fdip-00HI} gives invariance of
#{\operatorname{Ran}J} under #{S_t}. Since this range is closed, the derivative at
zero of #{S_tw} belongs to it whenever #{w\in D(H)\cap\operatorname{Ran}J}. Dividing
the displayed formula by #{\psi>0} shows that}

##{-\tfrac12 f''(x)-(\partial_x\log\psi)(x,y)f'(x)}

\p{is a function of #{x} alone, almost everywhere, for every such #{f}. On each
bounded interval choose #{f} with #{f'=1} there. Because #{\psi^2dq} is equivalent to
Lebesgue measure, Fubini shows that #{\partial_x\log\psi} is independent of #{y}
almost everywhere on that strip. Its continuity extends this to every #{(x,y)}, first
in #{y} on almost every vertical line, then in #{x} by density. Countably many
intervals cover #{\mathbb R}.}

\p{Thus #{\partial_x\log\psi(x,y)=b(x)} everywhere. Integrating in #{x} yields
#{\psi(x,y)=u(x)v(y)} with positive smooth #{u,v}. The eigen-equation then implies}

##{U(x,y)=E_0+\frac{u''(x)}{2u(x)}+\frac{v''(y)}{2v(y)}.}

\p{Its right side has zero mixed derivative, whereas #{\partial_x\partial_yU=-c\ne0},
a contradiction. This proves failure of the semigroup law for the interacting family,
without requiring an explicit ground-state formula. By \ref{fdip-00HI}, some
#{t>0} and some #{f\in L^2(\rho)} satisfy #{\|(I-E)S_tJf\|>0}.}

\p{When #{c=0}, the Hamiltonian and ground state factor into the two coordinates; the
retained subspace is invariant and the compressed semigroup is the ground-state
transform of the one-coordinate Hamiltonian. Interaction alone therefore does not
decide the issue: the coupling to the eliminated coordinate matters.}
